\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Observed retention capacity $R$ versus inferred permeability $k$ (Stage~I posterior mean) for the five packing media. The relationship is non-monotonic: both low-permeability media (pea gravels) and high-permeability media (medium pumice stones) achieve high retention, while moderate-permeability glass marbles exhibit the lowest retention. This pattern reinforces the friction-limited interpretation in which surface geometry and form drag, rather than hydraulic conductivity alone, govern sludge retention.}}{14}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:retention_vs_k}{{4}{14}{Observed retention capacity $R$ versus inferred permeability $k$ (Stage~I posterior mean) for the five packing media. The relationship is non-monotonic: both low-permeability media (pea gravels) and high-permeability media (medium pumice stones) achieve high retention, while moderate-permeability glass marbles exhibit the lowest retention. This pattern reinforces the friction-limited interpretation in which surface geometry and form drag, rather than hydraulic conductivity alone, govern sludge retention}{figure.4}{}}
